NAME
       cp - copy files and directories

SYNOPSIS
       cp [OPTION...] SOURCE DEST
       cp [OPTION...] SOURCE... DIRECTORY
       cp [OPTION...] -t DIRECTORY SOURCE...

DESCRIPTION
       cp copies SOURCE to DEST. When the last argument is an existing
       directory, every SOURCE is copied into it under its own name. An
       existing file named DEST is overwritten without a question unless you
       add -i or -n.

       By default cp does not copy directories (use -r or -a), the copy
       belongs to the user running cp, its mode is the source's mode limited
       by the umask, and its time stamps are new. The SELinux label of a new
       file comes from the directory it is created in, not from the source.
       Use -a, -p or --preserve when the copy must keep those attributes.

       On RHEL 9, root's shell has the alias cp='cp -i', so cp asks before
       overwriting. Use \cp or the full path /usr/bin/cp to skip the alias.

OPTIONS
       -r, -R, --recursive
              Copy directories and everything in them.

       -a, --archive
              Copy as exactly as possible: recursive, keep symbolic links as
              links, and keep the mode, owner, group, time stamps, ACLs,
              SELinux context and extended attributes. The same as -dR
              --preserve=all.

       -p     Keep the mode, owner and time stamps
              (--preserve=mode,ownership,timestamps).

       --preserve[=LIST]
              Keep the listed attributes: mode, ownership, timestamps, links,
              context, xattr, all.

       -i, --interactive
              Ask before overwriting an existing file.

       -n, --no-clobber
              Never overwrite an existing file.

       -f, --force
              If an existing destination cannot be opened, remove it and try
              again.

       -u, --update
              Copy only when the source is newer than the destination, or the
              destination is missing.

       -v, --verbose
              Print each file as it is copied.

       -l, --link
              Make hard links instead of copies.

       -s, --symbolic-link
              Make symbolic links instead of copies.

       -L, --dereference
              Copy the files that symbolic links point to.

       -P, --no-dereference
              Copy symbolic links as links.

       -t DIRECTORY, --target-directory=DIRECTORY
              Copy all SOURCE arguments into DIRECTORY.

       -T, --no-target-directory
              Treat DEST as a normal file, even if it is a directory.

       --parents
              Recreate each source's directory path under the destination.

       -b, --backup
              Keep a backup (DEST~) of a file that would be overwritten.

EXAMPLES
       Copy a file into a directory:

           cp /etc/hosts /root/backup/

       Copy a whole directory tree with owners, modes, ACLs and SELinux
       labels kept:

           cp -a /srv/data /srv/data.bak

       Copy a web page from the home directory, letting it take the label of
       /var/www/html:

           cp /home/student/index.html /var/www/html/

       Copy several files and say what happened:

           cp -v /etc/fstab /etc/hostname /root/saved/

       Keep the path when copying, giving /root/keep/etc/ssh/sshd_config:

           cp --parents /etc/ssh/sshd_config /root/keep

NOTES
       mv keeps a file's SELinux label (it is the same file); cp gives the copy
       a new one. After mv into a web or service directory, run restorecon.

SEE ALSO
       mv(1), rsync(1), install(1), ln(1), restorecon(8)
